WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
Faster walking pace links to lower cardiovascular and all-cause mortality in large UK Biobank cohort. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 178. A large group of participants in the United Kingdom provided walking speed records along with long-term health tracking. The records showed quicker walking tied to lower rates of death from heart conditions, as well as from any cause overall. The connection stood out even after researchers accounted for total amounts of physical activity.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:42.500
Open access charts display how mortality risk changed across different walking speeds. Now the focus shifts from broad population movement patterns to how daily food choices affect overnight rest.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:56.580
Days when people ate more fiber brought about 3% more deep sleep along with 3% more rapid eye movement sleep. Those same days also showed 2% less light sleep and a 2% drop in heart rate during rest.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:16.880
Meals that included many different plants along with foods rich in plant compounds produced similar improvements in sleep stages. Changes limited to single nutrients or vitamins over short periods failed to match those sleep shifts. The results point instead to effects that come from the full combination of foods rather than any one component.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:31.620
Next the discussion moves from daily habits that support cell upkeep to direct adjustments inside cells at the molecular level. Scientists created a way to modify ribosomes so each one focuses on building just one chosen protein.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:46.160
Normal cells require many thousands of different proteins at the same time and their ribosomes normally switch between very genetic messages. The engineered versions showed they could be adjusted to favor output of one protein type over others.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:57.100
The work so far has taken place only in laboratory cell cultures. After looking at how ribosomes can be adjusted, it helps to review how they normally manage many tasks at once.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:12.480
Most people assume ribosomes operate as identical machines that follow every incoming genetic message without preference. In practice, these cellular factories number in the millions inside a typical human cell and read messenger RNA strands in parallel.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:20.980
Each ribosome adds amino acids one by one to build protein chains while other ribosomes handle different instructions at the same moment.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:29.200
The recent experiments showed that the same core machinery can be altered to give priority to one protein while lowering production of others.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:39.160
Conditions that involve missing or excess proteins could one day be addressed by changing ribosomal behavior during translation rather than after proteins form.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:46.160
Tests in living organisms have not yet shown whether the changes can scale without harming overall cell operations.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.020
From control of protein output, the next topic turns to a new way to capture clearer images of those proteins.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:03.000
A computational technique now extracts finer structured detail from cryo-electron microscopy images without collecting extra high magnification frames.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:16.020
The method works around the usual limit set by detector pixel size and magnification level. It cuts down the time spent on the microscope and the amount of data stored while keeping the same number of particles per image.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:21.500
The improvement applies to structures of proteins, viruses, and larger molecular assemblies.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.760
From improved imaging tools, the next item covers engineered materials designed to match living tissue.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:36.540
Researchers at TU Delft developed a process that changes one mechanical trait in meta-bio materials while leaving others unchanged.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.380
Earlier approaches altered geometry in ways that affected several traits together.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:47.860
The new separation allows creation of implants that more closely copy the varied behaviors of natural tissues.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:54.700
From these engineered materials, the view widens to interactions between wild animals tracked across open land.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:01.120
Collars carrying global positioning devices recorded movements of lions and hyenas over wide areas.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.140
Male lions often move toward hyenas during the recorded encounters.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:09.140
Hyenas in turn moved away and gave ground in most cases.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:15.180
The patterns contradict the idea that hyenas only trail behind to pick up leftovers from lion kills.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:22.040
The track distances and speeds cover ranges far larger and faster than direct human observation could follow.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:29.780
People who frame experiences as mathematical cost benefit problems showed greater willingness to set aside moral rules.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.840
This pattern appeared in decisions involving money, status, or acceptance of immoral assistance.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.100
The association held after accounting for other individual differences.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.880
The study was published in the Sides Journal.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.740
New research in the Sides Journal examined how such mindsets relate to ethical compromises.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:53.600
Participants demonstrated this flexibility across various scenarios tested.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.540
The findings link calculated approaches to reduced adherence to ethical standards.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:06.960
A survey found nearly two-thirds of Australian children received at least one grooming-related message from a stranger online in the past year.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:12.100
Almost one-third reported direct requests for sexual information or interactions.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:16.240
The study documents the scale of exposure among young people.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.700
The results highlight widespread online interactions with potential risks.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.760
Data collection covered a representative sample of young Australians.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:28.820
Exposure included various forms of unsolicited contact.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:32.840
Researchers emphasized the need for greater awareness of these patterns.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:39.220
Submerged aquatic vegetation increased by 7% across the Chesapeake Bay in 2025.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.080
Gains were strongest in saltier waters.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:48.900
Researchers at William &amp; Mary's VIMS and Baton School tracked the changes as an indicator of ecosystem health.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.600
Submerged aquatic vegetation serves as one of the Chesapeake Bay's most important indicators of ecosystem health.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.400
The rise reflects improvements in water quality conditions.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.340
Monitoring efforts continue to assess long-term trends in the Bay.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:07.780
This positive change supports habitat for fish and other aquatic life.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:14.060
Three carbon rings create strange geometries that appear in many natural products and approved medicines.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:19.800
Their unusual shape supports applications ranging from antidepressants to antibiotics.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:25.300
Researchers continue exploring how these rings can be incorporated into new compounds.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:32.400
Cycle propanes are organic chemistry's smallest rings, with three carbon atoms joined in a triangle.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:36.500
The compact structure offers advantages in molecular design.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:40.340
Pharmaceutical development benefits from their unique properties.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.360
Ongoing work seeks to expand their use in therapeutic agents.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:54.020
The latest PISA assessment shows German students posting their weakest results in reading, mathematics and science, since testing began 25 years ago.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:00.900
The study measures how well young people can apply knowledge to real-life problems at the end of compulsory education.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:07.060
It demonstrates the extent to which young people are prepared for the demands of a knowledge-based society.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:11.600
Scores have declined across all three domains compared to previous cycles.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.360
This marks the lowest performance recorded for the country in the program's history.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.700
Educators note the challenges in preparing students for practical application of concepts.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.100
The assessment provides a benchmark for educational systems worldwide.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:33.120
Field surveys in August targeted fungal species in Norwegian alpine areas where dry-as-heath grows.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.880
Teams focused on locating and describing fungi that still lack formal scientific names.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.480
Work continues in remote mountain zones where fungal communities have received little prior study.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:51.160
Experts conducted field work in August to document fungal diversity in Norwegian alpine dry-as-heath.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:55.560
The effort focuses on species that have not yet been formally described.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.880
During some days in August, science takes center stage in these surveys of little known fungal diversity.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:06.520
Researchers aim to fill gaps in understanding of alpine ecosystems.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:14.400
Builders transported 25 tonne stones at least 18 kilometers to construct the devil's arrow's stone row in Britain.

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:18.920
The movement required deliberate planning and effort across difficult terrain.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:23.920
The row remains the tallest surviving prehistoric's own alignment in the country.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:32.780
New research has revealed that ancient builders deliberately transported enormous 25 tonne stones across at least 18 kilometers.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.860
The stones were moved over challenging landscapes.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:40.820
This feat indicates advanced organizational skills in prehistoric times.

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:44.580
The alignment stands as a significant archaeological feature.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:53.580
An interview study by Lingnan University's Asia Pacific Institute of Aging Studies examined how grandchildren support aging grandparents.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.880
Hong Kong's rapid population aging increases demand for family-based care.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:03.460
The work documents specific roles grandchildren assume in daily support.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:09.680
As Hong Kong's special administrative region's population ages at one of the fastest rates in the world,

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.840
demand for family-elder care increases daily.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.640
Grandchildren provide assistance in various household tasks.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:20.160
The study captures perspectives from multiple generations.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:23.920
Such support helps address gaps in formal care systems.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.860
Intense fires and landscape change in the ancient Southern Levant coincided with the emergence of pottery technology.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:38.260
The study suggests these conditions created opportunities for neolithic communities to observe how fire transforms clay.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:42.740
The link connects environmental upheaval with technological innovation.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:49.020
A new study suggests that a period of intense fires and landscape upheaval in the ancient Southern Levant

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:55.060
may have helped create the conditions that led neolithic communities to discover how fire could transform clay.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.420
The timing aligns with shifts in settlement patterns.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:02.160
Communities likely experimented with clay during these events.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.580
This discovery marked an important step in human technological history.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:13.740
Before we go, keep an eye on further tests of whether rewired ribosomes can operate safely inside living animals.

